Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 12:02:06 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] PRO-6096: Android crash report (1.58.0) (#11)

* fix(android): catch BackgroundServiceStartNotAllowedException (PRO-6096)

VoiceActivityProxy.handleIntent() called Context.startService() unconditionally
and uncaught when forwarding a call-related intent to VoiceService. On API 31+,
Android can reject a plain background startService() call with
BackgroundServiceStartNotAllowedException if the launching Activity's process
hasn't yet been registered as visible at that exact moment -- a timing race on
cold/background launches, not specific to one device (crash: Samsung Galaxy
S22, Android 16, v1.58.0/263).

Catch the exception on API 31+. ACTION_ACCEPT_CALL retries via
ContextCompat.startForegroundService() since VoiceService.acceptCall()
promotes itself to foreground immediately; other forwarded actions
(ACTION_FOREGROUND_AND_DEPRIORITIZE_INCOMING_CALL_NOTIFICATION) only post a
plain notification, so retrying those as foreground would trade this crash for
a guaranteed ForegroundServiceDidNotStartInTimeException -- log and drop
instead.

The exception-handling call is isolated in its own @RequiresApi(S) method so
verification of the newer class stays gated behind the SDK_INT check (minSdk
is 24).

* fix(android): catch ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException on retry (PRO-6096)

Per review nit on #11: the ACTION_ACCEPT_CALL fallback to
ContextCompat.startForegroundService() wasn't itself guarded, so the same
class of API-31+ rejection (ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException) could
still crash the retry path. Wrap it and log+drop for symmetry with the
primary startService() catch.

* refactor(android): catch IllegalStateException base class (PRO-6096)

Per review on #11: BackgroundServiceStartNotAllowedException extends
ServiceStartNotAllowedException extends IllegalStateException (API 1), so
catching the base class collapses the SDK_INT branch, the @RequiresApi helper
and both new imports into one plain try/catch -- and additionally covers
API 26-30, where the same background-start restriction throws a bare
IllegalStateException and was still uncaught (minSdk is 24). Same for the
startForegroundService() retry.

Also trimmed the javadoc's device/repro narrative, which duplicated the PR
description and PRO-6096; kept the ACTION_ACCEPT_CALL-vs-other-actions
rationale, which is the part that explains the code.

* fix(android): gate the foreground retry on actual promotion (PRO-6096)

The ACTION_ACCEPT_CALL retry via startForegroundService() assumed
VoiceService.acceptCall() always promotes itself, but it does not:
createOrReplaceForegroundNotification() no-ops with only a log line when
POST_NOTIFICATIONS is denied, and acceptCall() falls through and accepts the
call anyway, so startForeground() never runs. Denying notification permission
is a normal, reachable state -- VoiceActivityProxy's own permissionList already
treats POST_NOTIFICATIONS as a requestable runtime permission on API 33+.

In that state the retry traded this crash for
ForegroundServiceDidNotStartInTimeException, which the system throws
asynchronously once the promotion window expires, so the try/catch at the start
site could not intercept it -- exactly the failure mode the doc comment cites
as the reason not to retry the other actions.

Fix at the root rather than at the call site: VoiceService now exposes
canPromoteToForeground(), the single definition of whether a foreground start
will actually be honored, and gates its own promotion on it. VoiceActivityProxy
consults it before retrying and falls back to log-and-drop when promotion is
not possible -- same treatment as the other forwarded actions. One predicate,
two call sites, so the two cannot drift apart.

